  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "short": "Call everyone",
      "title": "Checkout calls everyone",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "When an order is placed, four other things must happen: send a confirmation email, reserve stock, record analytics, add loyalty points.\n\nThe order service calls each of them in turn and waits for every answer, so the shopper waits for all four — almost a second.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "Every new feature that cares about orders means changing, testing and redeploying the order service."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "checkout": "920 ms",
        "knows": "4 services"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "short": "Email down",
      "title": "Email is down, so nobody can buy",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "The email provider has an outage. The order service’s call to it fails — and because checkout waits on every call, **checkout fails**.\n\nA confirmation email, which could easily have been sent ten minutes later, just stopped all sales.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "With direct calls, availability multiplies: four services at 99.9% each give checkout about 99.6%."
      },
      "changes": [
        "- Email service down",
        "- Checkouts failing"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "checkout": "920 ms",
        "knows": "4 services",
        "breaks": "yes"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "em": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "short": "Publish",
      "title": "Publish an event instead",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "The order service now saves the order and **publishes** one event — `OrderPlaced` — to a topic on an [[event bus]], then answers the shopper straight away.\n\nEmail, inventory, analytics and loyalty each **subscribe** to the topic, and every one of them receives its own copy of the event.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Common brokers: Kafka, Amazon SNS + SQS, Google Pub/Sub, RabbitMQ (fanout exchanges), Redis Streams."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Event bus · topic “orders”",
        "~ 4 direct calls → 1 published event",
        "~ Checkout 920 → 140 ms"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "checkout": "140 ms",
        "knows": "1 event bus",
        "breaks": "no",
        "subs": "4"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "em": {
          "emphasis": true
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "short": "Queue vs topic",
      "title": "Queue or topic?",
      "chapter": "insight",
      "body": "In the last lesson, each job went to **one** worker — a queue shares out work. A topic does the opposite: each event goes to **every** subscriber — it spreads news.\n\nReal systems combine them: each subscribing service gets its own queue of events, shared by however many instances of that service are running.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Queue: “someone do this.” Topic: “this happened.”"
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "checkout": "140 ms",
        "knows": "1 event bus",
        "breaks": "no",
        "subs": "4"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "short": "New subscriber",
      "title": "New features just subscribe",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "The risk team wants to check every order for fraud. They build a service that subscribes to `OrderPlaced` — and that’s it.\n\nThe order service didn’t change, wasn’t redeployed, and doesn’t even know fraud checking exists. This is [[loose coupling]].",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Publishers don’t know their subscribers. Teams can add reactions to events without coordinating releases."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Fraud check subscribes",
        "~ Order service: no changes"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "checkout": "140 ms",
        "knows": "1 event bus",
        "breaks": "no",
        "subs": "5"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "short": "Catch up",
      "title": "A subscriber is down? It catches up",
      "chapter": "insight",
      "body": "Email is down again. This time checkout doesn’t notice: the event bus keeps email’s copies of the events in its subscription until it’s back.\n\nWhen email recovers, it works through the backlog and every confirmation goes out — a little late, but none lost.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Events are durable: a slow or broken subscriber delays only itself."
      },
      "changes": [
        "- Email service down",
        "+ Its events wait in its subscription",
        "~ Checkout unaffected"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "checkout": "140 ms",
        "knows": "1 event bus",
        "breaks": "no",
        "subs": "5"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "em": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "bem": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "short": "Trade-offs",
      "title": "What events cost",
      "chapter": "tradeoff",
      "body": "Events decouple services, but the flow becomes harder to see: “what happens when an order is placed?” is now spread across five codebases.\n\nSubscribers act a little later (eventual consistency), events can arrive twice or out of order, and debugging needs good tracing.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Name events as facts in the past tense (OrderPlaced, PaymentFailed), and make every subscriber idempotent."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "checkout": "140 ms",
        "knows": "1 event bus",
        "breaks": "no",
        "subs": "5"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "em": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "bem": {
          "emphasis": true
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "short": "Outbox",
      "title": "Case: saved, but never announced",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "The order service saves the order to its database, then publishes `OrderPlaced`. One day it crashes between those two steps: the order exists, but no event was sent. No email, no stock reserved, no loyalty points.\n\nFix: the [[transactional outbox]]. Write the event into an `outbox` table in the *same* database transaction as the order; a relay then publishes everything in the outbox to the bus.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Either both the order and its event are saved, or neither is. The relay retries until the bus accepts it — so subscribers must handle duplicates. It can poll the table, or tail the database’s own log (**change data capture**, e.g. Debezium)."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Orders DB with an outbox table",
        "+ Relay publishes from the outbox",
        "~ No more lost events after a crash"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "checkout": "145 ms",
        "knows": "1 event bus",
        "breaks": "no",
        "subs": "5"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "ob": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s9",
      "short": "Replay",
      "title": "Case: replaying history",
      "chapter": "insight",
      "body": "Analytics finds a bug that miscounted revenue for the last 30 days. With a classic queue, those events are gone once consumed.\n\nA log-based bus like Kafka **keeps** events for days or weeks. Analytics fixes its code, rewinds its position to 30 days ago, and reprocesses every event — other subscribers aren’t affected.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Retained events also let a brand-new subscriber build its data from history instead of starting empty."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Bus retains 30 days of events",
        "~ Analytics rewinds and reprocesses"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "checkout": "140 ms",
        "knows": "1 event bus",
        "breaks": "no",
        "subs": "5"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "an": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "bus": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "fr": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ob": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "bem": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "binv": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ban": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "bloy": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "bfr": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s10",
      "short": "Recap",
      "title": "Recap",
      "chapter": "recap",
      "body": "• Direct calls make the caller **slow** and **fragile**, and couple every team to it.\n• **Publish** an event; every **subscriber** gets its own copy.\n• New features **just subscribe**; down subscribers **catch up** later.\n• Pay for it with eventual consistency, duplicates and harder tracing.\n• Use an **outbox** so saves and events never disagree; retained logs allow **replay**.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Next up: **Real-time Updates** — getting events all the way to a user’s screen."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "checkout": "140 ms",
        "knows": "1 event bus",
        "breaks": "no",
        "subs": "5"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "an": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "bus": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "fr": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "ob": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
